Presidential elections were held in [[Mongolia]] on 26 June 2013. The [[Democratic Party (Mongolia)|Democratic Party]] nominee, incumbent [[President of Mongolia|President]] [[Tsakhiagiin Elbegdorj]] was re-elected, defeating both [[Mongolian People's Party]] nominee [[State Great Khural|of parliament member]] [[Badmaanyambuugiin Bat-Erdene]] and [[Mongolian People's Revolutionary Party (2010)|Mongolian People's Revolutionary Party]] nominee [[Natsagiin Udval]], who was Minister of Health at the time of the election. Elbegdorj was inaugurated on 10 July 2013 for his second term in office.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.shuud.mn/?p=252264|title=President to be sworn in on 10th in front of Genghis Khan monument|publisher=shuud.mn (in Mongolian)|date=3 July 2013|accessdate=6 July 2013|url-status=dead|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20130705082636/http://www.shuud.mn/?p=252264|archivedate=5 July 2013}}</ref>

Both Elbegdorj and Bat-Erdene saw backing from the urban population in [[Ulan Bator]], Bat-Erdene having more of a conservative following and the incumbent president supported by the middle class. Elbegdorj won the election with 50.9% of the [[Direct election|popular votes]], a nearly 9% margin over Bat-Erdene. Udval received 6.6% of the popular vote. Elbegdorj's re-election kept the Democratic Party in power both in the presidency and the [[State Great Khural]].

==Electoral system==
The election was held using the [[two-round system]]. If no candidate had received a majority of the vote in the first round, a second round would have been held. The second round was to be scheduled on 10 July 2013 if necessary.<ref>[https://web.archive.org/web/20130701085340/http://news.xinhuanet.com/english/world/2013-06/26/c_132486954.htm Mongolian presidential election starts] Xinhua, 26 June 2013</ref> Only parties with seats in the [[State Great Khural]] were allowed to nominate candidates.<ref>[http://www.electionguide.org/elections/id/2277/ Mongolia] IFES</ref>

The elections were the first in which Mongolians living abroad were allowed to vote,<ref>[http://electionguide.org/election.php?ID=2057 Election Profile] IFES</ref> with an estimated 39,800 Mongolian citizens living outside Mongolia at the time of the election. Other reforms had been made since the [[2012 Mongolian legislative election|2012 parliamentary elections]], including limits on money spent by candidates on campaigning and advertising. As in the 2012 elections, the presidential election used [[electronic voting machines]] from [[Dominion Voting Systems]].<ref name="unique features">{{cite news|last=Dari|first=E.|title=The unique features of the 6th Presidential Election of Mongolia|url=http://ubpost.mongolnews.mn/?p=4332|accessdate=27 June 2013|newspaper=UB Post|date=27 May 2013}}</ref>

==Campaign laws==
Changes to campaign laws were made in 2011 which restricted the amount of television and poster advertising a candidate could use. It also increased penalties for violators. The intent was to have the candidates judged on the merit of their [[Party platform|platform]] rather than the budget of their campaign. This change increased the role of [[social networking]] such as Facebook and Twitter in candidates campaigns.<ref name="unique features" />

==Candidates==
Three parties nominated candidates for the presidency.<ref name=UBPostCandidates>{{cite web|last=Dari|first=E.|title=Candidates for the Presidential Election announced|url=http://ubpost.mongolnews.mn/?p=4089|work=UB Post|accessdate=17 May 2013|archive-date=3 March 2016|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160303231430/http://ubpost.mongolnews.mn/?p=4089|url-status=dead}}</ref>

===Tsakhiagiin Elbegdorj===
{{main|Tsakhiagiin Elbegdorj}}
Incumbent president [[Tsakhiagiin Elbegdorj]] was renominated by [[Democratic Party (Mongolia)|Democratic Party]], and was also endorsed by [[Civil Will-Green Party]] and Mongolian National Democratic Party, both of which have seats in both the parliament and the government cabinet.<ref name=UBPostCandidates /> Republican Party and [[Motherland Party (Mongolia)|Motherland Party]] expressed their full support for Elbegdorj's candidacy also.<ref>{{cite news|title=Motherland Party and Republican Party are to support Ts.Elbegdorj|publisher=vip76.mn (in Mongolian)|url=http://vip76.mn/content/17526?rfr=10|accessdate=22 June 2013|date=29 May 2013|url-status=dead|archiveurl=https://archive.today/20130624234521/http://vip76.mn/content/17526?rfr=10|archivedate=24 June 2013}}</ref>

Elbegdorj was [[Prime Minister of Mongolia]] for two terms prior to his election as president in 2009. The incumbent president was "heavily backed by the urban middle class" of [[Ulaanbaatar]]. Elbegdorj was one of the leaders of the [[1990 Democratic Revolution in Mongolia|democratic revolution in 1990]] that saw the fall of the [[People's Republic of Mongolia]].<ref name=globe>{{cite news|last=Namjilsangarav|first=Ganbat|title=Mongolian President wins re-election|url=https://www.theglobeandmail.com/news/world/incumbent-mongolian-president-wins-2nd-term-on-pro-western-anti-graft-platform/article12850801/|accessdate=27 June 2013|newspaper=The Globe and Mail|location=Toronto|agency=Associated Press|date=26 June 2013|archive-date=27 June 2013|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20130627052448/http://www.theglobeandmail.com/news/world/incumbent-mongolian-president-wins-2nd-term-on-pro-western-anti-graft-platform/article12850801/|url-status=dead}}</ref>

===Badmaanyambuugiin Bat-Erdene===
{{main|Badmaanyambuugiin Bat-Erdene}}

The opposition [[Mongolian People's Party]] nominated [[State Great Khural|parliament member]] [[Badmaanyambuugiin Bat-Erdene]]. Bat-Erdene is a former [[Mongolian wrestling|wrestling]] champion and was the first wrestler to be announced as a presidential candidate in Mongolia.<ref name=UBPostCandidates /> Bat-Erdene had a more conservative backing within Ulan Bator. Mongolian People's Party was the former communist party in power prior to the [[1990 Democratic Revolution in Mongolia|democratic revolution in 1990]].<ref name=globe />

===Natsagiin Udval===
{{Main|Natsagiin Udval}}

The [[Mongolian People's Revolutionary Party (2010)|Mongolian People's Revolutionary Party]] was initially unsure if it was going to field a candidate in the election, or support another party's candidate. It eventually nominated [[Natsagiin Udval]] to run. Udval is the Minister of Health and the Secretary-General of Mongolian People's Revolutionary Party. She was also the first woman to run for the presidency.<ref name=UBPostCandidates /> Udval supports former president [[Nambaryn Enkhbayar]] who is in prison on corruption charges and who is chairman of [[Mongolian People's Revolutionary Party (2010)|Mongolian People's Revolutionary Party]].<ref name=globe />

==Results==
Incumbent President Elbegdorj won the election with 50.23% of total votes, with Baterdene Badmaanyambuu and Natsagiin Udval getting 41.97% and 6.5% respectively.<ref name=statesman>{{cite news|last=GANBAT|first=NAMJILSANGARAV|title=Incumbent wins Mongolia presidential election|url=http://www.idahostatesman.com/2013/06/26/2631894/voting-underway-in-mongolia-presidential.html|accessdate=27 June 2013|newspaper=Idaho Statesman (AP)|date=26 June 2013}}</ref><ref>{{cite news|title=Ts.Elbegdorj won by 50.23 percent of votes|publisher=shuud.mn|language=Mongolian|url=http://www.shuud.mn/?p=249992|accessdate=27 June 2013|date=27 June 2013|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20130629004536/http://www.shuud.mn/?p=249992|archive-date=29 June 2013|url-status=dead}}</ref>

Following the announcement Mongolian Prime Minister [[Norovyn Altankhuyag]] pledged work with President [[Tsakhiagiin Elbegdorj]] to fight corruption in Mongolia. A political analyst said having the parliament and presidency represented by a single party would be good for foreign investment, as it shows a "politically stable" environment.<ref name=globe />

